    {
        "publicationNumber": "10521",
        "doi": "10.1049/ic:20050433",
        "publicationYear": "2005",
        "publicationDate": "23-24 Nov. 2005",
        "articleNumber": "1574696",
        "articleTitle": "Alarms and abnormal situation management: experience from the process industries",
        "volume": null,
        "issue": null,
        "startPage": "177",
        "endPage": "204",
        "publisher": "IET",
        "articleContentType": "Conferences",
        "publicationTitle": "The 2005 IEE Water Event (Ref. No. 2005-11083)",
        "authors": [
            {
                "id": null,
                "preferredName": "P. Andow",
                "firstName": "P.",
                "lastName": "Andow"
            }
        ],
        "abstract": "The paper is a powerpoint presentation on the alarms and abnormal situation management (ASM) as experienced in process industries. The use of Operations Management Pro, jointly developed by Shell and Honeywell, in Shell refineries is discussed. The Experion enterprise model and its application parameters are presented. ASM consortium guidelines, the details of which are only available to ASM Consortium members, are outlined. The benefits of the use of ASM concepts, particularly of its good operator graphics, in some processing companies are presented.",
        "keywords": {
            "Index Terms": [
                "Risk Reduction",
                "Processing Unit",
                "Alarm System"
            ]
        }
    },
    {
        "publicationNumber": "10521",
        "doi": "10.1049/ic:20050427",
        "publicationYear": "2005",
        "publicationDate": "23-24 Nov. 2005",
        "articleNumber": "1574685",
        "articleTitle": "Application of network optimisation tools to real time control of water distribution networks",
        "volume": null,
        "issue": null,
        "startPage": "89",
        "endPage": "92",
        "publisher": "IET",
        "articleContentType": "Conferences",
        "publicationTitle": "The 2005 IEE Water Event (Ref. No. 2005-11083)",
        "authors": [
            {
                "id": null,
                "preferredName": "R. Burd",
                "firstName": "R.",
                "lastName": "Burd"
            }
        ],
        "abstract": "This presentation considers the use of hydraulic modelling tools to predict the performance of distribution networks. Telemetry can then be used to compare actual performance compared with predicted performance, thus allowing systems to be optimised and anomalies in network performance to be more rapidly detected.",
        "keywords": {
            "Index Terms": [
                "Distribution Network",
                "Network Operators",
                "Control Room",
                "Demand Forecasting",
                "Real Control",
                "Hydraulic Model",
                "Unplanned Events"
            ]
        }
    },
    {
        "publicationNumber": "10521",
        "doi": "10.1049/ic:20050434",
        "publicationYear": "2005",
        "publicationDate": "23-24 Nov. 2005",
        "articleNumber": "1574697",
        "articleTitle": "Decision support [water supply industry]",
        "volume": null,
        "issue": null,
        "startPage": "205",
        "endPage": "219",
        "publisher": "IET",
        "articleContentType": "Conferences",
        "publicationTitle": "The 2005 IEE Water Event (Ref. No. 2005-11083)",
        "authors": [
            {
                "id": null,
                "preferredName": "R. Millet",
                "firstName": "R.",
                "lastName": "Millet"
            },
            {
                "id": null,
                "preferredName": "J. Fenwick",
                "firstName": "J.",
                "lastName": "Fenwick"
            }
        ],
        "abstract": "The paper is a powerpoint presentation on the application of a decision support system in the improvement of the control room functions of Yorkshire Water Services Ltd. The control room stores records on water assets, monitors water assets, stores customer contacts, provides fast information about operational incidents, uses SAP to combine finance, asset and workflow in one system, and generally manages water production and distribution. The decision support for the control room includes: a video wall to display the location and frequency of customer calls, display the latest weather report, alarm throughput, alarms that have not been handled, and the site operating cost; modelling tools in cases of pipeline or pumping station failure, low-level reservoir, pressure and flow incidents, storm impacts; and data searches during alarms which include contact details, previous history, reportable incidents and operational notes.",
        "keywords": {
            "Index Terms": [
                "Decision Support"
            ]
        }
    },
    {
        "publicationNumber": "10521",
        "doi": "10.1049/ic:20050423",
        "publicationYear": "2005",
        "publicationDate": "23-24 Nov. 2005",
        "articleNumber": "1574674",
        "articleTitle": "Challenges and opportunities in AMP4 [water supply industry]",
        "volume": null,
        "issue": null,
        "startPage": "1",
        "endPage": "15",
        "publisher": "IET",
        "articleContentType": "Conferences",
        "publicationTitle": "The 2005 IEE Water Event (Ref. No. 2005-11083)",
        "authors": [
            {
                "id": null,
                "preferredName": "M. Tempest",
                "firstName": "M.",
                "lastName": "Tempest"
            }
        ],
        "abstract": "This is a powerpoint presentation on the challenges and opportunities in AMP4 in Thames Water. Investment has been dominated by water quality and environmental improvements since the privatisation of RWE Thames Water in 1989. The balance for investment priorities include: renewing ageing water mains and sewers; reducing the number of households at risk from sewer flooding; minimising odours from sewage treatment works; investing in planning sustainable new water resources; and complying with EU legislation. The Thames Water AMP4 capital programme is up c20% on AMP3. However, since skills and competence of the workforce and those of industry partners is vital to achieving its priorities, there is concern over the ability to resource and retain experienced water industry operatives and technicians. Automation and control systems and information integration with IT systems are also major concerns of Thames Water.",
        "keywords": {
            "Index Terms": [
                "IT Systems",
                "Efficiency Savings"
            ]
        }
    },
    {
        "publicationNumber": "10521",
        "doi": "10.1049/ic:20050430",
        "publicationYear": "2005",
        "publicationDate": "23-24 Nov. 2005",
        "articleNumber": "1574690",
        "articleTitle": "Telemetry standards for the water industry: an update",
        "volume": null,
        "issue": null,
        "startPage": "127",
        "endPage": "133",
        "publisher": "IET",
        "articleContentType": "Conferences",
        "publicationTitle": "The 2005 IEE Water Event (Ref. No. 2005-11083)",
        "authors": [
            {
                "id": null,
                "preferredName": "B. Bartindale",
                "firstName": "B.",
                "lastName": "Bartindale"
            }
        ],
        "abstract": "For the past two years, a team representing most of the major water and environment organisations in the UK together with key system suppliers and consulting engineers has been working to establish a common standard for the communication of telemetry data for the water industry. This has resulted in the definition of a protocol based on the internationally-recognised DNP3 standard. An update is provided and ways are suggested to engage with (and benefit from) this exciting initiative.",
        "keywords": {
            "Index Terms": [
                "Industrial Water",
                "Project Management",
                "Communication Protocol",
                "Telemetry System"
            ]
        }
    },
    {
        "publicationNumber": "10521",
        "doi": "10.1049/ic:20050429",
        "publicationYear": "2005",
        "publicationDate": "23-24 Nov. 2005",
        "articleNumber": "1574688",
        "articleTitle": "Is this WAP we've been waiting for? Using smart mobile technology in the field",
        "volume": null,
        "issue": null,
        "startPage": "119",
        "endPage": "126",
        "publisher": "IET",
        "articleContentType": "Conferences",
        "publicationTitle": "The 2005 IEE Water Event (Ref. No. 2005-11083)",
        "authors": [
            {
                "id": null,
                "preferredName": "D. Boyle",
                "firstName": "D.",
                "lastName": "Boyle"
            },
            {
                "id": null,
                "preferredName": "J. Robbins",
                "firstName": "J.",
                "lastName": "Robbins"
            },
            {
                "id": null,
                "preferredName": "S. Williams",
                "firstName": "S.",
                "lastName": "Williams"
            }
        ],
        "abstract": "This paper provides a brief overview of WAP technology and how it has been used in SCADA/telemetry applications over recent years. A practical example is given within British Waterways (BW) where 'smart' mobile technology is being exploited to improve efficiency and customer service while reducing operational risks. An overview of the development and security issues together with typical operational costs is also presented.",
        "keywords": {
            "Index Terms": [
